.TH CCOL_DYNMQ_DISABLE_SENDING 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynmq_disable_sending \- turn off sends on a dynamic queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_dynmq_disable_sending(ccol_dynamic_queue *dq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Marks the queue as closed to new sends, so every later send returns
ccol_not_permitted until somebody calls ccol_dynmq_enable_sending(). A
refused send keeps the ownership of its own msg->data.

A dynamic queue grows on demand, so no sender ever waits for room the
way it does on a circular queue, and there are no blocked senders to
wake here.

While its sends are off, the queue counts as ready for the write
direction, the same way a pipe whose reader has gone is ready for
POLLOUT. For that reason the call wakes every write-direction waiter on
the queue, meaning a ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call or
a ccol_event_loop registration. A ccol_select() call returns the index
of the queue, and the ccol_dynmq_send_zc() that the caller makes next
returns ccol_not_permitted at once. A ccol_event_loop registration gets
one on_writable call, and a write registration added while sends are
off gets one as soon as it is added. After that the registration is
called again only for a later event on the queue, such as a receive or
another change of the send state, so it doesn't spin. A write waiter
that starts while sends are off returns at once too.

Receives are not affected. The messages already in the queue stay
there and are received the normal way, so this is the natural way to
close the producer side and let a consumer drain whatever is left.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dq   Dynamic queue that gets sends turned off
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       - Sends are off
ccol_invalid_args  - dq is NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
To turn sends back on, call ccol_dynmq_enable_sending(). The function
is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_dynmq_enable_sending (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_msg_count (3),
.BR ccol_circq_disable_sending (3)
